{% endif %}
{% if use_caddy %}
In production, Caddy fronts `{{ domain_name }}` and handles HTTPS automatically
(Let's Encrypt) — see `Caddyfile`. It runs standalone, independent of
`docker compose up` above:

When upgrading an existing Caddy-enabled project from a template version where
Caddy ran in the application Compose project, remove the old proxy before
starting the standalone stack so its ports are free. From the project
directory, run `docker compose down --remove-orphans` (this stops the
application services too; do not add `-v`, which would delete volumes). The
old Caddy volumes are retained. Find their names with `docker volume ls`;
they are usually `<old-project>_caddy_data` and
`<old-project>_caddy_config`. Copy their contents to the standalone stack's
volumes to preserve certificates and ACME account data:

```bash
docker volume create caddy_caddy_data
docker run --rm -v OLD_CADDY_DATA:/from -v caddy_caddy_data:/to alpine \
  sh -c 'cp -a /from/. /to/'
docker volume create caddy_caddy_config
docker run --rm -v OLD_CADDY_CONFIG:/from -v caddy_caddy_config:/to alpine \
  sh -c 'cp -a /from/. /to/'
```

Replace `OLD_CADDY_DATA` and `OLD_CADDY_CONFIG` with the old volume names you
found above. Then start the standalone stack:

```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.caddy.yml up -d
```

See `docs/architecture.md` if you're running more than one project from this
template on the same host — only one of them should start
`docker-compose.caddy.yml`. Locally, `docker-compose.override.yml` publishes
the app's port directly instead, since Caddy's automatic HTTPS needs a real
public domain and inbound 80/443.
{% endif %}

## Local (non-Docker) dev

```bash
uv sync --all-extras --dev
uv run pre-commit install
uv run python manage.py migrate
uv run python manage.py runserver
```

If you want local email capture on that path, run `docker compose up mailpit` in a
second terminal. The generated settings default SMTP to `localhost:1025`, which the
compose `mailpit` service publishes on the host, and its web UI is at
`http://localhost:8025`.

## Tests

```bash
uv run pytest
```

## Background tasks
{% if use_redis %}
Tasks are defined with `django_tasks.task()` (see `apps/{{ initial_app_name }}/tasks.py`)
and run on the RQ backend against Redis. A `worker` service in `docker-compose.yml` runs
`manage.py rqworker default --job-class django_tasks_rq.backend.Job`. In tests, the
`ImmediateBackend` runs tasks synchronously so no Redis is needed in CI.
{% else %}
Tasks are defined with `django_tasks.task()` (see `apps/{{ initial_app_name }}/tasks.py`)
and run synchronously in-process via `ImmediateBackend` — no Redis or worker process
needed. This is fine for simple apps, but it blocks the request while the task runs.
Regenerate this project with `use_redis=true` to switch to the RQ-backed async backend.
{% endif %}
This project uses `django-tasks` (the Django 6.0 `django.tasks` backport) so the
migration path to native Django 6.0 LTS task support (~April 2027) should mainly involve
an import-path swap.


